# ROLLBACK-FLOOR — the oldest STILL-SUPPORTED server binary a CURRENT database
# must run: the floor of the version range a rollback is allowed to land in.
#
# Rolling the server back is a real operation: deploy/auto-deploy.sh swaps a new
# binary in, and when the new one fails its health check the previous binary is
# put back and pointed at the SAME SQLite file the new one has already opened,
# migrated and written to. Nothing in `go test ./...` covers that direction.
# Every migration test in server/account runs one binary forward over an old
# file; not one of them ever hands a NEWER file to an OLDER binary, which is the
# only direction a rollback actually takes.
#
# This file names the exact commit that binary is built from, and
# scripts/test/db-rollback-harness.sh builds it — the real binary, from these
# exact git objects — and drives one shared database through
# floor -> HEAD -> floor -> HEAD.
#
# ## Format
#
# Blank lines and lines whose first character is `#` are ignored. Exactly ONE
# significant line must remain, and it must match, byte for byte:
#
#     commit <40 lowercase hex digits>
#
# Anything else is a parse failure, not a default: see parseRollbackFloor in
# server/rollback_floor_test.go, which is the authoritative reader. The harness
# applies the same rule independently in shell so a floor that only one of them
# can read fails both.
#
# ## What this commit is
#
# 7b3c6f4973e19ade7cd7bfa1699c745b60accf86 is the oldest rollback baseline this
# repository still supports — the bottom of the supported range, not a mirror of
# whatever is deployed at this moment. It was chosen because it is
# PRODUCTION-VERIFIED: it is a commit that really ran in production, so a
# rollback landing on it is an operation that can actually happen rather than a
# hypothetical version gap.
#
# It carries the billing-deletion journal shape (server/account/billing_deletion.go),
# the metered upload path (server/account/files.go) and the usage_periods /
# usage_monthly accounting semantics (server/account/sqlite.go), which are the
# three places where a database written by a newer binary could plausibly stop
# an older one.
#
# ## It may lag production, and lagging is the safe direction
#
# This line is NOT kept in step with the deployed commit, and production moving
# forward is not by itself a reason to touch it. Deploys happen continuously;
# the supported rollback floor changes far more rarely, so this record is
# expected to sit behind production, potentially by a long way.
#
# That gap is conservative rather than risky. A floor OLDER than the binary an
# operator would really be put back onto makes the harness exercise a strictly
# LONGER version gap: it can go red on an incompatibility no real rollback would
# ever meet, but it cannot go green on one a real rollback would hit. An
# over-aggressive floor has the opposite, dangerous asymmetry. When in doubt,
# leave this line alone.
#
# ## Advancing it
#
# Raising this line is its own deliberate change, never a side effect of a
# deploy and never bundled into the change that made the gate go red. All three
# of the following must hold:
#
#   1. A NEWER COMMIT IS ESTABLISHED AS THE ACTUAL ROLLBACK BASELINE — not
#      merely "production moved past the old one", but that the newer commit is
#      genuinely the oldest binary an operator would now be rolled back onto and
#      everything below it is out of support. Until that is true of a specific
#      newer commit, this line stays exactly where it is.
#   2. THE ADVANCE IS A LATER CANDIDATE CHANGE. It lands as its own reviewed
#      change on top of the work that established the new baseline, so the move
#      can be judged on its own evidence.
#   3. THE CANDIDATE'S HEAD STAYS DIFFERENT FROM IT. A floor equal to the tree
#      under test is not a rollback at all; the harness refuses it outright
#      (both binaries would hash the same) and so should any reviewer.
#
# The commit must also be an ancestor of HEAD and must contain a buildable
# server/ tree; the guards refuse it otherwise.
#
# ## The one edit this record exists to forbid
#
# Moving this line to make a FAILING rollback pass. A floor chosen so the gate
# goes green proves nothing about the binary an operator would really roll back
# to — it deletes the evidence instead of producing it. If the harness goes red,
# the finding is the incompatibility it just discovered, never a newer floor.
commit 7b3c6f4973e19ade7cd7bfa1699c745b60accf86
